{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Hom.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.bar_even_reciprocal_sq_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.bar_even_reciprocal_sq_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InversionKillsEvenElliptic.lean#L133-L147","doc":"**The bar-even reciprocal-odd σ-unitary factor squares to one.** If the σ-reflective\nfactor is additionally σ-fixed (bar-even, `σ(e x) = e x`), then `e(−x) = e x` (even), so\nwith reciprocity `e x ^ 2 = 1`. Thus inversion + σ-unitarity exclude the **bar-even** part\nof the reciprocal-odd residual algebraically; the remaining non-σ-fixed part is what the\ngrowth/boundedness route (`BoundedMeromorphicLiouville`) removes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.sigma_eq_reflection_of_reciprocal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.sigma_eq_reflection_of_reciprocal","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InversionKillsEvenElliptic.lean#L120-L131","doc":"**σ-unitary + reciprocal-odd ⇒ σ-reflective.** A σ-unitary factor (`σ(e x)e x = 1`)\nthat is reciprocal-odd (`e x · e(−x) = 1`, the inversion residual) satisfies\n`σ(e x) = e(−x)`: complex conjugation acts as argument reflection. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.factor_sigmaUnitary_of_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.factor_sigmaUnitary_of_mul","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InversionKillsEvenElliptic.lean#L107-L118","doc":"**The connecting factor inherits σ-unitarity.** If both `Φ` and the modified\nsolution `e · Φ` are σ-unitary (`σ(Φ)Φ = 1`, `σ(eΦ)(eΦ) = 1`), then `e` is σ-unitary\n(`σ(e x)·e x = 1`): the elliptic factor connecting two pure phases is itself a pure\nphase. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.even_factor_eq_one_or_neg_one_of_inversion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.even_factor_eq_one_or_neg_one_of_inversion","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InversionKillsEvenElliptic.lean#L76-L90","doc":"**Hence an even inversion-compatible factor is `±1`-valued** (integral domain). A\nnonconstant elliptic (continuous) function is not `±1`-valued, so inversion excludes the\neven elliptic freedom — the generic `℘` factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.even_factor_sq_eq_one_of_inversion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.even_factor_sq_eq_one_of_inversion","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InversionKillsEvenElliptic.lean#L64-L74","doc":"**An even inversion-compatible factor squares to one.** If additionally `e` is even\n(`e(−x) = e(x)`), reciprocity `e(x)·e(−x) = 1` collapses to `e(x)² = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.elliptic_factor_reciprocal_of_inversion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.InversionKillsEvenElliptic.elliptic_factor_reciprocal_of_inversion","kind":"theorem","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/InversionKillsEvenElliptic.lean#L46-L62","doc":"**Inversion forces the surviving factor to be reciprocal-odd.** If `Φ` satisfies the\nFaddeev inversion `Φ(x)·Φ(−x) = c` and the modified solution `e · Φ` satisfies the *same*\ninversion, then `e(x)·e(−x) = 1` at every `x` (`c ≠ 0`). So the elliptic factors compatible\nwith inversion are exactly the reciprocal-odd ones — a proper subgroup of the full elliptic\ngroup that the shift equations alone allow. ","signatureChunk":0}]}